window.docContents[221].resolve({"/Axioms/#print-axioms":{"contents":"The command #print axioms, followed by a defined identifier, displays all the axioms that a definition transitively relies on.\nIn other words, if a proof uses another proof, which itself uses an axiom, then the axiom is reported by #print axioms for both.\n\nThis can be used to audit the assumptions made by a proof, for instance detecting that a proof transitively depends on the sorry tactic.def lazy : 4 == 2 + 1 + 1 := by sorry\n#print axioms lazy\n'lazy' depends on axioms: [sorryAx]\nPrinting Axioms of Simple DefinitionsConsider the following three constants:def addThree (n : Nat) : Nat := 1 + n + 2\ntheorem excluded_middle (P : Prop) : P ∨ ¬ P := Classical.em P\ntheorem simple_equality (P : Prop) : (P ∨ False) = P := or_false P\nRegular functions like addThree that we might want to actually evaluation typically do not depend on any axioms:#print axioms addThree\n'addThree' does not depend on any axioms\nThe excluded middle theorem is only true if we use classical reasoning, so the foundation for classical reasoning shows up alongside other axioms:#print axioms excluded_middle\n'excluded_middle' depends on axioms: [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]\nFinally, the idea that two equivalent propositions are equal directly relies on propositional extensionality.#print axioms simple_equality\n'simple_equality' depends on axioms: [propext]\nUsing #print axioms with #guard_msgsYou can use #print axioms\ntogether with #guard_msgs to ensure\nthat updates to libraries from other projects cannot silently\nintroduce unwanted dependencies on axioms.For example, if the proof of double_neg_elim below changed in such a way that it used more\naxioms than those listed, then the #guard_msgs command would report an error.theorem double_neg_elim (P : Prop) : (¬ ¬ P) = P :=\n  propext Classical.not_not\n\n/--\ninfo: 'double_neg_elim' depends on axioms:\n  [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]\n-/\n#guard_msgs (whitespace := lax) in\n#print axioms double_neg_elim\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean 中文参考手册\u0009Axioms","header":"8.5. Displaying Axiom Dependencies","id":"/Axioms/#print-axioms"},"/Basic-Types/Integers/#Lean-__________________--Basic-Types--Integers--API-Reference--Arithmetic":{"contents":"Typically, arithmetic operations on integers are accessed using Lean's overloaded arithmetic notation.\nIn particular, the instances of Add Int, Neg Int, Sub Int, and Mul Int allow ordinary infix operators to be used.\nDivision is somewhat more intricate, because there are multiple sensible notions of division on integers.\n\nAddition of integers, usually accessed via the + operator.This function is overridden by the compiler with an efficient implementation. This definition is\nthe logical model.Examples:* (7 : Int) + (6 : Int) = 13* (6 : Int) + (-6 : Int) = 0\n\nSubtraction of integers, usually accessed via the - operator.This function is overridden by the compiler with an efficient implementation. This definition is\nthe logical model.Examples:* (63 : Int) - (6 : Int) = 57* (7 : Int) - (0 : Int) = 7* (0 : Int) - (7 : Int) = -7\n\nNon-truncating subtraction of two natural numbers.Examples:* Int.subNatNat 5 2 = 3* Int.subNatNat 2 5 = -3* Int.subNatNat 0 13 = -13\n\nNegation of integers, usually accessed via the - prefix operator.This function is overridden by the compiler with an efficient implementation. This definition is\nthe logical model.Examples:* -(6 : Int) = -6* -(-6 : Int) = 6* (12 : Int).neg = -12\n\nNegation of natural numbers.Examples:* Int.negOfNat 6 = -6* Int.negOfNat 0 = 0\n\nMultiplication of integers, usually accessed via the * operator.This function is overridden by the compiler with an efficient implementation. This definition is\nthe logical model.Examples:* (63 : Int) * (6 : Int) = 378* (6 : Int) * (-6 : Int) = -36* (7 : Int) * (0 : Int) = 0\n\nPower of an integer to a natural number, usually accessed via the ^ operator.Examples:* (2 : Int) ^ 4 = 16* (10 : Int) ^ 0 = 1* (0 : Int) ^ 10 = 0* (-7 : Int) ^ 3 = -343\n\nComputes the greatest common divisor of two integers as a natural number. The GCD of two integers is\nthe largest natural number that evenly divides both. However, the GCD of a number and 0 is the\nnumber's absolute value.This implementation uses Nat.gcd, which is overridden in both the kernel and the compiler to\nefficiently evaluate using arbitrary-precision arithmetic.Examples:* Int.gcd 10 15 = 5* Int.gcd 10 (-15) = 5* Int.gcd (-6) (-9) = 3* Int.gcd 0 5 = 5* Int.gcd (-7) 0 = 7\n\nComputes the least common multiple of two integers as a natural number. The LCM of two integers is\nthe smallest natural number that's evenly divisible by the absolute values of both.Examples:* Int.lcm 9 6 = 18* Int.lcm 9 (-6) = 18* Int.lcm 9 3 = 9* Int.lcm 9 (-3) = 9* Int.lcm 0 3 = 0* Int.lcm (-3) 0 = 0\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean 中文参考手册\u0009Basic Types\u0009Integers\u0009API Reference","header":"20.2.4.3. Arithmetic","id":"/Basic-Types/Integers/#Lean-__________________--Basic-Types--Integers--API-Reference--Arithmetic"},"/Basic-Types/Maps-and-Sets/#Lean-__________________--Basic-Types--Maps-and-Sets--Extensional-Hash-Maps--Iteration":{"contents":"Updates the values of the hash map by applying the given function to all mappings.\n\n","context":"Lean 中文参考手册\u0009Basic Types\u0009Maps and Sets\u0009Extensional Hash Maps","header":"20.19.4.5. Iteration","id":"/Basic-Types/Maps-and-Sets/#Lean-__________________--Basic-Types--Maps-and-Sets--Extensional-Hash-Maps--Iteration"},"/Definitions/Recursive-Definitions/#mutual-partial-fixpoint":{"contents":"Lean 支持使用 偏不动点 来定义 互递归 函数。\n互递归既可以通过 互递归块 引入，也可能来自 let rec 表达式和 where 代码块。\n带偏不动点的互递归规则，会应用到由互递归组的精译步骤所得、经过提升后且实际上互相递归的一组定义上。\n\n若互递归组中的所有函数都带有 partial_fixpoint 子句，就会采用这一策略。\n\n","context":"Lean 中文参考手册\u0009定义\u0009递归定义\u0009偏不动点递归","header":"7.6.4.4. 偏不动点下的互递归","id":"/Definitions/Recursive-Definitions/#mutual-partial-fixpoint"},"/Introduction/#typographical-conventions":{"contents":"本文档采用了多种排版和布局方式，来表示不同方面的信息。\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean 中文参考手册\u0009简介","header":"1.2. 排版方式","id":"/Introduction/#typographical-conventions"},"/Notations-and-Macros/Extending--do--Notation/#Lean-__________________--Notations-and-Macros--Extending--do--Notation--Elaboration":{"contents":"Elaboration of do-elements occurs in the DoElabM monad.\nThis monad is a wrapper around TermElabM that provides one extra reader value: a do-elaboration context.\nThe elaborators also receive an additional argument: a description of the elaboration continuation.\nThe continuation represents the remainder of the do-block, after the current element; it includes both a DoElabM action that will elaborate the remainder of the block and the name by which that term will refer to the result of the current elaboration step.\nUnlike term elaborators, which return the elaborated term to a surrounding elaboration context, do-element elaborators invoke the provided continuation to arrange for the elaboration of the rest of the do-block.\n\nInferred and cached information about the monad.The mutable variables in declaration order. Kept in sync with mutVarDefs; insertions go\nthrough declareMutVar / declareMutVars only.Maps mutable variable names to their MutVar record. Kept in sync with mutVars.The expected type of the current do block.\nThis can be different from ReturnCont.resultType in for loop do blocks, for example.Information about return, break and continue continuations.Whether the current do element is dead code. elabDoElem will emit a warning if not .alive.Pluggable builders for pure and bind applications.\n\nThe inferred type of the monad of type Type u → Type v.The u in m : Type u → Type v.The v in m : Type u → Type v.The cached PUnit expression.The cached PUnit.unit expression.\n\nWhether a code block is alive or dead.We inferred the code is syntactically dead and don't need to elaborate it at all.We inferred the code is semantically dead, but we need to elaborate it to produce a program.The code is alive. (Or it is dead, but we failed to prove it so.)\n\nTo avoid circularity in the implementation, the Context.contInfo and Context.ops fields are references that are filled in after construction.\nUse ContInfoRef.toContInfo and DoOpsRef.toDoOps to recover the underlying data:\n\n\n\nInformation about a success, return, break or continue continuation that will be filled in\nafter the code using it has been elaborated.\n\n\n\nPluggable builders for the pure / bind applications emitted by the do elaborator.Build pure (α:=α) e : m α.Build bind (α:=α) (β:=β) e k : m β.If e is syntactically a pure … application, return the pure value; otherwise none.\nUsed by DoElemCont.mkBindUnlessPure to contract e >>= pure to e and\npure e >>= k to let x := e; k x.Match a monad application m α, returning MonadInfo for m and α.Construct m α from α.\n\nElaborators are associated with syntax kinds using the doElem_elab attribute.\nThey should have type DoElab.\nIn addition to an elaborator, each custom do-element that's implemented via an elaborator must be provided with control information.\n\nThe type of elaborators for do block elements.It is elabTerm `(do $e; $rest) = elabDoElem e dec, where elabDoElem e · is the elaborator for\ndo element e, and dec is the DoElemCont describing the elaboration of the rest of the block\nrest.\n\nDo Element ElaboratorsRegisters a do element elaborator for the given syntax node kind.A do element elaborator should have type DoElab (which is\nLean.Syntax → DoElemCont → DoElabM Expr), i.e. should take syntax of the given syntax node kind\nand a DoElemCont as parameters and produce an expression.When elaborating a do block do e; rest, the elaborator for e is invoked with the syntax of e\nand the DoElemCont representing rest.The elab_rules and elab commands should usually be preferred over using this attribute\ndirectly.\n\nAdditionally, elab_rules can be used to simultaneously define an elaborator and associate it with syntax.\nJust as elab_rules : term <= ty binds the expected type to ty, elab_rules : doElem <= dec binds the continuation to dec.\n\nJust as term elaborators may recursively invoke elaboration on their sub-terms by calling functions such as elabTerm, do-element elaborators may elaborate nested do-elements or sequences of do-elements.\nTo elaborate a single do-element, call elabDoElem.\nTo elaborate a non-empty array of do-elements, call elabDoElems1.\nTo elaborate a sequence of do-elements, call elabDoSeq.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean 中文参考手册\u0009Notations and Macros\u0009Extending  do -Notation","header":"23.7.3. Elaboration","id":"/Notations-and-Macros/Extending--do--Notation/#Lean-__________________--Notations-and-Macros--Extending--do--Notation--Elaboration"},"/releases/v4.30.0/#Lean-__________________--Release-Notes--Lean-4___30___0-_LPAR_2026-05-26_RPAR_--Highlights--cbv--Tactic-Expansion":{"contents":"The cbv tactic, introduced in v4.29.0, is no longer experimental and receives major new capabilities in this release.\n\ncbv performs a process similar to call-by-value evaluation in order to simplify or close the goals.\n\ndef fact : Nat → Nat\n| 0 => 1\n| n+1 => (n+1) * fact n\n\ndef pow2 : Nat → Nat\n| 0 => 1\n| n+1 => 2 * pow2 n\n\n-- `simp` requires providing functions\nexample : fact 5 < pow2 7 := by simp [fact, pow2]\n-- `cbv` just executes directly\nexample : fact 5 < pow2 7 := by cbv\n\n\nv4.30.0 introduces the following improvements:\n\n* #12597: cbv_simproc system mirroring simp's simproc infrastructure.* #12773: at location syntax (cbv at h, cbv at h |-, and cbv at *).* #12788: set_option cbv.maxSteps N for user-configurable step limits.* #12763: short-circuit evaluation for Or/And: for expressions like decide (m < n ∨ expensive)* Other improvements: #12851 / #12944 / #12875 / #12888.\n\n","context":"Lean 中文参考手册\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.30.0 (2026-05-26)\u0009Highlights","header":"cbv  Tactic Expansion","id":"/releases/v4.30.0/#Lean-__________________--Release-Notes--Lean-4___30___0-_LPAR_2026-05-26_RPAR_--Highlights--cbv--Tactic-Expansion"}});